rationale:
**Verdict.** Pipeline and field tie at 43; model scores 39. Under my lens (codebase fit and simplicity) pipeline wins the tie: it fits the repo best and has the fewest dependencies. The synthesis should take field's offline and field UX and model's validation approach.

**How I checked.** I read all six understand/*.md files and the research notes, then checked claims against the worktree source (HEAD 69858dc), origin/main and the live catalog.

**Claims confirmed in source (these apply to all three designs):**
- `config.py` has only the `catalogs/` 60 s rule and the immutable `tiles/` and `vectors/` rules, and no content types for `.tif`, `.gz` or `.pmtiles`. New trails/routing prefixes go before the default.
- `b2.py` has no list API and no ContentEncoding. `exists` uses head_object. `get_file` exists, and `delete_prefix` is list plus delete_objects.
- `health.publish` is a best-effort read-modify-write (`get_json` then `put_json`) on the shared `catalogs/health.json`.
- In `cli.py`, `common` is a closure inside `build_parser` (lines 1500-1509), so `register(sub, common)` works.
- `useMapLayerSync` has the MANAGERS array and the INTERACTIVE list at :289-292.
- `zOrder.ts`: BELOW_LABELS ends at the `rd-national-perimeters` sentinel.
- `routeLayer` is a single Feature with casing `#0d0a0c` and line `#4aa3ff`.
- `store.ts` `selectFire` rebuilds the whole directions object (with a stale `picking: null`).
- `SearchDirectionsControl.tsx` fetches every profile in parallel, keyed on `endpointsKey` (:353, :374-407), and the profile-switch path only knows `'failed'` (:409-422).
- The `packs.ts` wrapper never reads headers and passes URLs it does not index straight to rawFetch (:128-166).
- The `opfs.ts` extension regex is `png|json|geojson|tif|tar|pdf`.
- `vite.config.ts` has no `worker` key.
- `HealthView` reads the 40 most recent runs across all workflows (:29).
- `index.html` sets only `upgrade-insecure-requests` as CSP, so the S3 endpoint and module workers are not blocked.
- The live catalog has 321 fires, 9 of them with bare cornea_ids (e.g. `4883092e-…`) and 37 in AK/HI/PR. This confirms pipeline's `fire_key` claim.

**Pipeline, specifically.** Its claims held up:
- register-pattern CLI;
- pointers under `catalogs/`;
- tile.yml-style PIPESTATUS output and 4-shard matrix;
- apt osmium-tool (noble 1.16) with a stdlib OPL parser;
- numpy, justified because the apt GDAL numpy can't be imported under `uv run`;
- the catalog-404-as-CORS-TypeError detail;
- no Range bypass needed because the national archive is never packed;
- `LayerContext` untouched, with only two MANAGERS entries.

It also has the smallest dependency set and the least churn in the offline wrapper, thanks to the BufferSource approach.

Its weaknesses:
- The B2 infrastructure is heavy: plan→`work/osm` intermediates, `osm_index`, run markers, `fires.json`, version-deleting prune, a custom ArcGIS pager, and MVT sampling in `pmtiles_inspect`.
- Automated `--confirm` pruning runs from cron, which the repo's deletion convention forbids.
- No endpoint snapping.
- A soft ×50 perimeter penalty instead of a hard block with an explicit override.
- One overclaim: a `test_config.py` test cannot enforce that immutable keys are never rewritten at runtime.

**Field.** It is the best offline field design:
- auto-on trails offline, plus OSM ways in the extract;
- a routing-area outline;
- explicit offline states for Drive and Apparatus;
- specific per-mode errors;
- endpoint snapping;
- sliced, cancellable search;
- window-local search arrays;
- an index built from pointers only;
- manual-only prune.

Its costs:
- a PyPI pyosmium dependency, justified on an inaccurate claim about osmium-tool;
- a tri-state trails mode that departs from the repo's boolean toggles;
- a mutable `trails/latest.json` outside `catalogs/`;
- more layers and managers, plus a bigger diff in the contested `SearchDirectionsControl`;
- scope creep (hotspot proximity checks, Walk here, routing-area manager);
- 3D-distance time costs.

**Model.** It has the best routing fidelity and validation: parity fixtures against a Python reference A*, golden routes, the horizontal-distance convention, and reuse of `buildLut`. But it is the most complex design:
- separately hashed parts with carry-forward and trail-part reuse;
- a 3DEP source added;
- a 100 km AOI (about 11M cells);
- a daily cadence;
- actions/cache keys that include run_id and will thrash the 10 GB cache;
- an unacknowledged health race;
- every subcommand added inline in `cli.py`.

**Stale base, affects all three.** origin/main is now 8 commits past 69858dc:
- Dropped pin (9ab4f1a, c40f05c) adds `pinDrop.ts` `FEATURE_LAYERS` and `clicksClaimed`.
- NWCG draw (0f2239b) renamed the `rd-draw-*` ids.
- `ensureOrder` has been rewritten, and `rd-hotspot-flames` added.

Every design treats `FEATURE_LAYERS` as a conditional "after merge" step. It is now required.

I judged for field correctness, safety and offline behaviour. The field design is the only one that is faithful to GET v2 where it matters for safety. It applies the isotropic Lorentz rate on terrain slope (SlpD), so steep sidehill travel is penalized, and adds a round-trip-preserving uphill/downhill asymmetry; I verified the α values numerically.

Pipeline and model both put directional grade into the GET term. I computed that this makes contouring across a 35° timbered slope about 3× faster than GET: 58 vs 182 min/km. Their routes would favour steep sidehills and report optimistic escape times.

Field also has the most complete set of failure modes and labels for a crew with no signal:
- endpoint snapping within 150 m;
- a hard perimeter block with a 60 m standoff that also covers graph nodes, with explicit behaviour when an endpoint is inside the perimeter;
- PERIM_OLD, HOTSPOT_NEAR and ONLINE_NO_PERIM warnings;
- explicit offline states for drive and apparatus;
- an OSM 'ways' layer in the pack, the only ground reference on the basemap-less offline style;
- window-local search memory and cancellable sliced search;
- a 60 m fallback so mega-fires stay routable.

Its NHD title filter is the only correct one of the three. I checked against the captured TNM JSON: 12 of 29 items match 'HU) 8', and 0 match the pipeline/model strings.

Pipeline has the best infrastructure: catalog.json as the fire list, separate health docs, content-addressed check/skip, osmium extracts made once in the plan stage, perennial-only lakes, count-verified paging and a BufferSource for offline trails. But as specified it has safety defects. Its perimeter penalty is applied only to grid moves, so trails through the fire are unpenalized and unflagged. It uses a 0 m buffer and a soft ×50 penalty with a possibly false 'no way around' message. It has no endpoint snapping, so pins on >45° or water cells get a false 'unreachable'. And its HU8 filter matches nothing.

Model has the best perimeter-blocked UX, the per-pixel LF mosaic and the strongest validation plan. It shares the directional-slope and HU8 bugs, and adds phone-memory risk from 11M-cell AOIs gated on navigator.deviceMemory (which iOS lacks) and a daily cadence.

Recommended synthesis:
- Take field as the base for routing, UX and offline behaviour.
- Graft pipeline's worker infrastructure: the catalog.json fire list, catalogs/health/*.json, content-addressed bundle_id with a check action, the plan-stage osmium extracts in work/, perennial-only lakes, Python paging with count checks, CPL_DEBUG degradation counting, and the BufferSource extract.
- Graft model's per-pixel LF mosaic, blocked_by_perimeter alternative, parity and golden-route validation, OSM-topology-preserving conflation, T5/T6 exclusion and range-first display.
